 \ReviewerComment{Section 2: missing literature on CERRA reanalysis. Since CERRA is available at a spatial resolution of \textasciitilde{}5 km over Europe, it is worth considering at least to complete the literature on reanalysis and satellite products.}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for this suggestion. We have added the Copernicus European Regional Reanalysis (CERRA) to the reanalysis literature in Section~2 (Related work), citing \citet{ridal2024cerra}. The added text states that CERRA provides reanalysis data over Europe at a horizontal resolution of 5.5~km, finer than the global ERA5 reanalysis. It also notes that \citet{ridal2024cerra} reported improvements over ERA5 for several near-surface variables. The present study uses ERA5 for both predictors and target precipitation; it does not compare the precipitation forecasting performance of CERRA and ERA5, and we do not infer that the finer resolution of CERRA would improve the regional forecasts evaluated here. Regarding satellite products, Section~2 already discusses two evaluations of satellite-based precipitation estimates \citep{palharini2021assessment,kumar2025long}, which show that their performance depends on location, terrain and rainfall intensity.}
 
-\ChangeLocation
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~2 (Related work), reanalysis paragraph: two sentences on CERRA inserted after the discussion of ERA5 precipitation evaluations and before the satellite-product studies. One bibliography entry added \citep{ridal2024cerra}. Final page and line numbers pending full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Added CERRA literature (Section~2, Related work; final page and line numbers pending)}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The Copernicus European Regional Reanalysis (CERRA) provides atmospheric reanalysis data over Europe at a horizontal resolution of 5.5~km, offering finer spatial detail than the global ERA5 reanalysis \citep{ridal2024cerra}. Although \citet{ridal2024cerra} reported improvements over ERA5 for several near-surface variables, the present study uses ERA5 for both predictors and target precipitation and does not assess whether CERRA would improve regional precipitation forecasting.}
+}
 
