 \section{Related work}
 
-Recent advances in meteorological modeling have been supported by the increasing availability of high-resolution reanalysis datasets and the development of machine learning techniques, both of which have become important for precipitation forecasting, as demonstrated by \citet{10512739}. Reanalysis datasets provide temporally and spatially consistent climate information that supports retrospective climate studies and weather prediction applications \citep{10512483}. Among these products, the ERA5 reanalysis dataset developed by ECMWF under the Copernicus Climate Change Service represents a substantial improvement over its predecessors. \citet{hersbach2020era5} introduced ERA5 with hourly coverage and a spatial resolution of approximately 31~km, showing marked improvements over ERA-Interim in reproducing variability and extremes across multiple atmospheric variables. Subsequent evaluations have further clarified the reliability and limitations of ERA5. \citet{mcnicholl2021evaluating} found strong agreement between ERA5 near-surface air temperature and ground-based observations but also reported discrepancies under complex meteorological conditions. \citet{doddy2021reanalysis} compared several reanalysis products for renewable energy applications in Ireland and showed that ERA5 generally outperforms MERRA-2 and JRA-55, while still exhibiting regional biases that require consideration. For precipitation, \citet{lavers2022evaluation} evaluated 24-hour totals against 5,637 stations over 2001--2020, finding a general wet bias, lower annual maximum one-day amounts, and smaller random errors in winter than summer in the extratropics. These daily-scale findings do not establish the accuracy of hourly precipitation over Dublin. \citet{palharini2021assessment} evaluated eleven satellite-based precipitation products across different regions of Brazil and found that no single product consistently captured extreme rainfall, with performance varying by region and event intensity. Similarly, \citet{kumar2025long} evaluated CHIRPS and GSMaP\_ISRO rainfall estimates against gauge observations over India during 2000--2022 and found that GSMaP\_ISRO agreed more closely with station measurements than CHIRPS, while both products diverged notably over complex terrain such as the Western Ghats. These studies show that precipitation products can provide useful regional information, but their performance remains dependent on location, terrain, and rainfall intensity.
+Recent advances in meteorological modeling have been supported by the increasing availability of high-resolution reanalysis datasets and the development of machine learning techniques, both of which have become important for precipitation forecasting, as demonstrated by \citet{10512739}. Reanalysis datasets provide temporally and spatially consistent climate information that supports retrospective climate studies and weather prediction applications \citep{10512483}. Among these products, the ERA5 reanalysis dataset developed by ECMWF under the Copernicus Climate Change Service represents a substantial improvement over its predecessors. \citet{hersbach2020era5} introduced ERA5 with hourly coverage and a spatial resolution of approximately 31~km, showing marked improvements over ERA-Interim in reproducing variability and extremes across multiple atmospheric variables. Subsequent evaluations have further clarified the reliability and limitations of ERA5. \citet{mcnicholl2021evaluating} found strong agreement between ERA5 near-surface air temperature and ground-based observations but also reported discrepancies under complex meteorological conditions. \citet{doddy2021reanalysis} compared several reanalysis products for renewable energy applications in Ireland and showed that ERA5 generally outperforms MERRA-2 and JRA-55, while still exhibiting regional biases that require consideration. For precipitation, \citet{lavers2022evaluation} evaluated 24-hour totals against 5,637 stations over 2001--2020, finding a general wet bias, lower annual maximum one-day amounts, and smaller random errors in winter than summer in the extratropics. These daily-scale findings do not establish the accuracy of hourly precipitation over Dublin. \revminor{The Copernicus European Regional Reanalysis (CERRA) provides atmospheric reanalysis data over Europe at a horizontal resolution of 5.5~km, offering finer spatial detail than the global ERA5 reanalysis \citep{ridal2024cerra}. Although \citet{ridal2024cerra} reported improvements over ERA5 for several near-surface variables, the present study uses ERA5 for both predictors and target precipitation and does not assess whether CERRA would improve regional precipitation forecasting.} \citet{palharini2021assessment} evaluated eleven satellite-based precipitation products across different regions of Brazil and found that no single product consistently captured extreme rainfall, with performance varying by region and event intensity. Similarly, \citet{kumar2025long} evaluated CHIRPS and GSMaP\_ISRO rainfall estimates against gauge observations over India during 2000--2022 and found that GSMaP\_ISRO agreed more closely with station measurements than CHIRPS, while both products diverged notably over complex terrain such as the Western Ghats. These studies show that precipitation products can provide useful regional information, but their performance remains dependent on location, terrain, and rainfall intensity.
 
 Parallel to advances in reanalysis data, machine learning has gained prominence in precipitation forecasting. \citet{latif2023assessing} reviewed rainfall prediction models and concluded that machine learning methods, particularly recurrent neural networks such as LSTMs, generally outperform traditional statistical approaches, although challenges related to robustness and interpretability remain. Expanding on this, \citet{rahman2022rainfall} developed a fusion-based system for smart-city rainfall prediction that combines multiple classifiers with fuzzy logic, reporting improvements in prediction accuracy in their study. With the development of deep learning, more sophisticated spatiotemporal architectures have been explored. \citet{adewoyin2021tru} proposed TRU-NET, which incorporates attention mechanisms and ERA5 inputs to produce high-resolution rainfall predictions over the UK. Their model outperformed U-Net baselines and produced competitive results against dynamical forecasts. In their UK daily-rainfall evaluation, the TRU-NET CC variant underpredicted observed amounts above 20~mm\,day$^{-1}$ more than IFS. This daily-scale comparison does not establish the behaviour of the models evaluated here for Dublin regional-mean hourly precipitation. At the global scale, \citet{singh2021deep} trained a residual U-Net on reanalysis data in a cubed-sphere framework and reported improved correlation skill over an operational numerical weather prediction system, while also emphasizing the need for hybrid integration and probabilistic evaluation. \citet{miller2021analysis} examined short-term and seasonal rainfall forecasts in Kenya and found substantial regional differences in forecasting skill, highlighting the importance of localized validation. These studies demonstrate the potential of machine learning and deep learning for precipitation forecasting, while also showing that aggregate performance may not fully represent model behaviour under different rainfall conditions.
 
